<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zhu Xi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1130">1130</a>–<a href="/years/1200">1200</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zhu Xi was a Chinese philosopher of the Southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song Dynasty</a> and the leading figure of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neo-confucianism">Neo-Confucianism</a>. His synthesis of Confucian ethics and metaphysics, and his commentaries on the Four Books, later became the basis of the imperial civil service examinations. His thought became the intellectual foundation of East Asian governance and social organisation for six centuries.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zhu-xi.jpg" alt="Zhu Xi" width="320" height="360" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 向史公哲曰 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%94%AF%E4%B8%80%E5%8F%AF%E9%9D%A0%E7%9A%84%E6%9C%B1%E7%86%B9%E7%94%BB%E5%83%8F.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
